WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.392 --> 00:00:07.653
Everybody, "When We Walk with the Lord."

00:00:07.653 --> 00:00:11.307
♪ When we walk with the Lord ♪

00:00:11.307 --> 00:00:13.440
♪ In the light of his word ♪

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.490
It's Sunday in Lagos, Nigeria.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.946
And it's time for church.

00:00:18.946 --> 00:00:23.196
♪ What a glory he sheds on our way ♪

00:00:24.453 --> 00:00:26.880
I've come to this delightfully neon house

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.480
of worship with a certain
degree of reluctance,

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.250
but when one of Africa's tech titans

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.820
invites you to his church, you say yes.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.700
I have to warn you, I come
from a long line of atheists.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.500
I'm not-
It's okay.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.333
I'm not good at church.

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:42.437
That is exactly who we need at church.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.010
Just experience it and
then that's it, you know.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:47.843
No miracles.

00:00:49.123 --> 00:00:52.044
♪ Your love is so wonderful ♪

00:00:52.044 --> 00:00:54.963
♪ Your love is so wonderful ♪

00:00:54.963 --> 00:00:57.829
♪ It's so high, I can't get over it ♪

00:00:57.829 --> 00:00:58.695
♪ It's so low, I can't get under it ♪

00:00:58.695 --> 00:00:59.528
Like everything here,

00:00:59.528 --> 00:01:01.443
the service is high energy,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.913
enough energy to make a nonbeliever smile.

00:01:07.913 --> 00:01:10.779
♪ Nobody, nobody ♪

00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:13.497
♪ Nobody shall like Jesus ♪

00:01:13.497 --> 00:01:16.549
♪ Nobody, nobody ♪

00:01:16.549 --> 00:01:19.216
♪ Nobody, I say ♪

00:01:38.359 --> 00:01:41.220
What I tell you or even show you next

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.053
will not do Lagos justice.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.880
This is a city you have to feel

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.080
if you're to have any
hope understanding it.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:57.840
More than 26 million people,
every single one of them,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.233
striving and selling constantly.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.080
For a laid-back Californian,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.593
the energy here is exhilarating
and sometimes unnerving.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.190
I've never seen anything quite like it.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:14.130
I am here at the largest market in Lagos.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:18.060
As you can see, it's
barely controlled chaos.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.220
It's just block after block after block

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.893
selling everything you
can possibly imagine.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.420
It's like this just every day?

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.290
Yep.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.750
What, everybody just comes
here to get all kinds of stuff.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.663
Anything, literally almost
anything at best prices.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.200
Michael here is a local entrepreneur

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.030
and solver of problems

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.370
and my much needed guide to the market.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.890
We're on this street,
which is pretty busy,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.210
but I mean, there's streets in there

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.100
where there's like, no cars.
Busy.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.170
Just wall-to-wall people.
Cars can't even get through,

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.700
so the whole market, you
literally have to squeeze

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.850
your way through, so it's pretty intense.

00:03:01.944 --> 00:03:04.830
You can hear, they keep chanting
"oyinbo, oyinbo, oyinbo."

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.390
That's white man, white man, white man.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.273
That's what they're saying?
Yeah.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.620
That kissing sound, is
that considered rude

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.744
or that's just like
standard operated procedure?

00:03:15.744 --> 00:03:17.938
It's standard operated procedure

00:03:17.938 --> 00:03:21.030
to get someone's attention.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.280
Seems like a hard way
to make a living, man.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.880
It is, yes, but some people
make good money here as well.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.320
Yeah?

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.360
You get it from the lowest prices

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.210
and you sell it for however
it smart you can sell it for.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.820
Locals will be quick
to tell you that Lagos

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.130
is the beating heart of Nigeria

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:47.253
and that Nigeria is the
beating heart of Africa.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.530
And they'll also tell you that if,

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.490
if Lagos could fix some
things, its infrastructure,

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.133
it's corruption, it's
old ways of thinking,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.840
well, then there's a
historic economic revolution

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.500
at the ready, led by a nascent tech scene

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:08.733
that is ready to boom.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.930
And no one wants to see this
happen more than this man,

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.240
who most people call E.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.340
My full name is Iyinoluwa
Aboyeji but most people

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.690
can't pronounce it so everybody
calls me E, just the letter.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:24.573
I'm the founding partner of
the Fund for Africa's Future.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.390
Africa has seven tech startups

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.030
valued at more than $1 billion

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:34.440
and E started two of them,
Andela and Flutterwave.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.160
He's often hailed as the
Zuck or Musk of Nigeria,

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.810
although, in this case,
that's meant as a compliment.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.763
Starting a startup here was not easy.

00:04:46.763 --> 00:04:47.596
I heard something about you guys

00:04:47.596 --> 00:04:48.780
getting kicked out of offices.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:50.430
Oh man, that's the stories for days.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.690
What was going on?

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.400
Most people assumed if you had a computer

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.890
you were a Yahoo boy.
Yeah.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.860
Is Yahoo boy, is that a
that's a Nigerian name?

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.560
That's a Nigerian term.
Okay.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.270
That's why is this the
scams people always-

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:07.103
Scams, yeah.

00:05:07.103 --> 00:05:09.490
So people who do scams,
we call them Yahoo boys.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.100
Okay.
Yeah.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.390
No disrespect to Yahoo.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.960
I should probably take that
off and say something else.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.480
We're actually in the part of Lagos

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.700
where you grew up, I think, right?

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:27.533
I lived here.

00:05:27.533 --> 00:05:28.737
But what is this part called?

00:05:28.737 --> 00:05:29.687
It's called Yaba.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.896
How close is this to where
you actually grew up?

00:05:33.896 --> 00:05:34.729
It's not far.

00:05:34.729 --> 00:05:37.020
I could walk, my house where I was born

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.660
is maybe a 45 minute walk from here

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.180
and I went to primary school

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.250
literally right over those rail tracks.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:45.870
Okay.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.140
Could we have a little
drive around just see?

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:48.270
Yeah, we'll drive around, we'll see.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:49.590
The haunts from your childhood.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.203
See everything, yeah.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.710
The population of Lagos has exploded

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.243
over the past 20 years.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.020
The result is unplanned, unzoned,

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.393
put anything anywhere you can, havoc.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.140
That said, much of the business and wealth

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.510
is clustered on Victoria Island,

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:14.340
which people describe as
the Manhattan of Lagos.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.900
Even though he is well off now,

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.990
he very purposely rejected
living on the island

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.560
to stick to his youthful
and tech roots in Yaba.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.420
Would you say this is the ground zero

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.030
of tech to startups, the talents?

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.500
The talent, yeah, it's cheapest,

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.790
best place to live.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.190
So kind of where all the startups,

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.963
the dot-coms hanging out.
Yeah.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.950
The startups are here in large part

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.140
because of the local university.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.350
So we are on the campus
of University of Lagos,

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.740
which is one of the most
amazing universities.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.530
And that's why Yaba is in such proximity

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.260
because the talent found
is easier to waft over.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.270
Yeah, so there's a nice interplay between

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.070
that part of town and the university.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.390
Yeah, exactly.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:58.350
A lot of youth here.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.900
Yeah, getting ready to go do big things.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:05.760
But to do big things in
Lagos, you have to master

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:07.083
how Lagos works.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.830
And learn how to navigate
its unique flavor

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.690
of business customs.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.360
When I was at Adela, people
didn't know how we did it,

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.610
but we had 300 kids.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.670
They were walking there with 300 MacBooks

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.770
each worth $1,000 dollars back

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.290
and forth every day in this neighborhood.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:30.150
People were like, "How did you do that?"

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.000
But it was in the neighborhood watch.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.430
Neighborhood watch in this case refers

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.880
to the Lagosian phenomenon of Area Boys.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.160
These are people who control neighborhoods

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.283
in an unofficial but very real capacity.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:49.023
One word to describe the
Area Boys might be mafia.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.013
This sounds bad and
there's some truth to it.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.420
Many people here though
don't really trust the police

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:00.810
to begin with.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.420
And the Area Boys are a more local force

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:04.653
for maintaining order.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:10.083
The trick is to learn how to
operate within the system.

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:12.360
I give their leader a title

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:13.650
as was my chief security officer,

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.980
I give him a badge, gave him a package,

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.590
including health insurance
for all these guys

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:21.090
and we're good.

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.800
We made sure everybody was safe.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.630
The whole neighborhood was safe.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.957
But if you don't know Nigeria,

00:08:26.957 --> 00:08:30.593
it can be very hard to do business.

00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:33.410
E's business savvy showed
itself early in life.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:39.000
As a kid, he helped
his mom hawk stock fish

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.950
and ice blocks in the streets

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:44.117
and he discovered that
he was pretty good at it.

00:08:44.117 --> 00:08:45.180
I just picked up entrepreneurship

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:46.710
skills very, very easily.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:49.620
I imagine it came from
ultimately being the kind

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.812
of kid who really liked money.

00:08:51.812 --> 00:08:53.605
Like money.
I loved money.

00:08:53.605 --> 00:08:54.536
I love money.

00:08:54.536 --> 00:08:55.380
I remember when I was growing up,

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.354
I wanted to be a policeman

00:08:58.354 --> 00:08:59.730
'cause I saw they were the only ones

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:01.283
who were taking money from the drivers.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:05.070
I didn't understand then that
they weren't supposed to.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:08.910
And my dad made a very,
very significant decision.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:13.230
His children would all go
to the best possible school,

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:16.710
even if he couldn't ordinarily afford it.

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:19.830
He went to Canada to
continue his education.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.740
In the tech hotspot of Waterloo, Ontario,

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:25.710
he and a few friends
discovered their calling.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:29.760
You had a little Nigerian
crew in Canada at Waterloo.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:31.410
Yeah, pulled my people with me.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.450
I started to tell my
Nigerian people, look,

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.880
there's a lot more to this
school than just getting

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:43.740
the degree and staying here
trying to get a passport.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.930
At the time our motivations
weren't so noble.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:48.280
I mean wanted to just
make money for ourselves.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:51.930
He convinced his friends

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:55.110
to abandon their secure
career paths in North America,

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:58.203
come back to Nigeria and
try to make it big in tech.

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:03.330
In 2014, they started Andela, which aimed

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:06.450
to train up a new generation
of Nigerian coders

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:08.853
and get them remote gigs
from around the world.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.000
It was the right idea at the right time

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.030
and it became a smashing success.

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:18.120
That allowed E to start his
payments company, Flutterwave,

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:19.980
another smashing success.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.260
If somebody would've
told you you would create

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:24.660
two billion companies, would you?

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:25.493
Never believe.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:28.170
Well, you know, one thing I'll say.

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:31.170
I did have faith that we
would do something big.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:33.870
I just didn't know it was
going to be this big this soon.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.420
Nigeria's tech scene
has been on a good run,

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:50.160
but its long-term prospects
face the same challenges

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.993
as other industries in the country.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.010
The power infrastructure
is a terrible mess.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.410
There are blackouts almost daily

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:01.203
and people rely on diesel
generators to get by.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:06.033
It's roads and accompanying
traffic and just as bad.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:10.620
The political system
is in constant turmoil.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.983
Few people I talk to trusted
any political regime.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.750
In general, conditions are unstable

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.400
and it's hard to get things done.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:25.170
Everyone I talk to here is
optimistic about the future,

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:27.870
but people always point
back to the infrastructure

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:31.680
not really changing much or
or upgrading for decades.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:34.770
The reality is our
infrastructure has been this way

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:38.880
for ages because we have
lacked for a long time

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:40.730
the investment to make it any better.

00:11:43.874 --> 00:11:46.740
When I talk to American
capital, I think this,

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:51.660
the images of the starving
kid with a fly on their nose

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:56.460
is so stuck in their heads
that actually it's a lot easier

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:59.190
to have a conversation with
them about philanthropy

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.873
than it is about investment.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:06.460
He knows of what he speaks.

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:10.230
He has gone to the heart of VC land

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:13.200
and was rather curiously
dismissed in spite

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:15.840
of his multi-billion dollar track record.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:18.660
I sort of arrived Silicon Valley thinking

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:23.223
that what I'd done in Africa
would matter, but it didn't.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:26.940
That's kind of twisted
because if somebody had done

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:28.413
the same thing in the US,

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.680
people would be falling all over them.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.230
It is a different
standard, to some extent.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.430
But I think it's better

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:38.850
because we've now learned
to be very self-assured

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:40.863
and confident in what we do here.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.570
Uninterested in waiting
for foreign capital

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.180
to find Nigeria and Africa,

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.643
E has taken matters into his own hands.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:57.690
His Future Africa Fund has invested

00:12:57.690 --> 00:12:59.493
in about 50 startups here.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:03.180
One is a company called
Releaf, which is trying

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:06.153
to modernize the business
of Nigerian farming.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:12.230
It was co-founded in 2017
by this fella, Ikenna Nzewi.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:16.710
He's Nigerian by background,
but was raised in the US.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:19.350
Everything here comes
with a negotiation like,

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:20.220
like every step.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:21.750
Small price, yeah.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:22.770
How did you get used to this

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.780
or how did you figure this all out?

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:25.860
I wasn't used to it at first.

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:26.693
Or have you yet?

00:13:26.693 --> 00:13:27.526
I wasn't used to it at first.

00:13:27.526 --> 00:13:28.812
I started meditating.

00:13:28.812 --> 00:13:31.863
It actually did me a lot of good.

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:36.930
You guys have, you've
got this mix of machines

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:39.570
and then software and technology.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:41.940
Maybe should we start with the Kraken?

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:43.058
Yeah, let's start with Cracken.

00:13:47.649 --> 00:13:48.690
The Cracken is basically

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:50.940
a really well-made nutcracker.

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.283
Sadly, no tentacles are involved.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:14:00.210
The idea is to automate some
of the production of palm oil,

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:02.703
a process that's often done by hand here.

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:05.760
So when you're cracking open the nuts,

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:08.460
you want to apply enough force
that you crack the shell,

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:11.360
but not that you crack the
kernel that's within the shell.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.190
It's kind of like this perfect medium

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:16.090
that you have to find between the two.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:20.400
And like true tech try hards,

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:24.630
they used satellites and
AI to locate new customers.

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:26.310
This really funky map,

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:30.340
that is basically our ability
to see farms from space

00:14:31.233 --> 00:14:33.120
and then go and meet the farmers.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:36.900
So to do this, some AI was
just trained up to be able

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.656
to spot the pattern of
the shape of the palm.

00:14:39.656 --> 00:14:41.550
Exactly, those triangles shaped trees.

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:43.120
Everywhere the orange touches

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.463
is where an oil palm farm is.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:57.150
What called you back to the country

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:59.820
and called you to do
farming in particular?

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:02.040
I think it was a mix of like my passion

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:04.920
for seeing economic development in Africa

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.400
and also just the period of
time that we are living in.

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:11.340
Africa's really in the
period where it can boom

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:13.020
and so this is actually the point in time

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:16.110
to create solutions that will
expand into a large piece

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:18.060
of market share over the next 10 years.

00:15:24.384 --> 00:15:25.890
Other startups in E's portfolio

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:28.920
are more like your typical
Silicon Valley joint.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:32.970
Think app-based delivery
services for urban professionals,

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.883
though with a particularly Lagosian twist.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.360
Eden Life is one example.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:43.770
The company was started by
Nadayar Enegesi, a BFF of E.

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:47.430
He's one of the original crew
of Nigerians who met in Canada

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:50.400
and became the tech kings of Lagos.

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:53.310
You became like the, maybe I'm wrong,

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:54.960
it seems like the Nigerian equivalent

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:58.260
of the PayPal mafia or
something like that, right?

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:01.020
You started so many
companies are you guys-

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:03.093
Are we like celebrities?

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:08.133
No, no, we're not, we're not, we're not.

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:14.400
After helping build Andela with E,

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:16.833
Nad set out on his own with Eden Life.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:19.290
The company delivers food

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:21.990
and does a smattering of
other chores like laundry

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:25.380
and gardening for busy
middle-class Nigerians.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:29.100
It's these services of day-to-day
life to free up more time

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:31.020
for you to do whatever
you want to be doing.

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:32.610
Is that the basic idea?

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:34.410
Yes, exactly, exactly.

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:36.630
Everybody is like tremendous talent.

00:16:36.630 --> 00:16:38.790
Everybody is working
harder and harder every day

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:39.623
to try to make it,

00:16:39.623 --> 00:16:41.430
but there's different
roadblocks in the way.

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:43.693
For example, if you're
living in a place like Lagos

00:16:43.693 --> 00:16:44.760
and you're going to work every day,

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.663
you're probably spending like
five to six hours in traffic.

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:56.493
He's not kidding about the traffic at all.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:01.680
To get to Victoria Island

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:04.140
and its sweet, sweet opportunities,

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:06.783
people have to cross
this bridge every day.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:11.013
It's two hours one way, if you're lucky.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:16.290
Eden Life aims to help people
claw back some of that time,

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.930
and ultimately to become the go-to app

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:20.970
for all manner of services.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:22.470
We describe what we are building

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:24.390
as the operating system for life

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:27.333
where you can find all of
these things in one place.

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:30.660
Nad certainly has Silicon Valley speak

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:34.740
down cold, though personally,
I'm not sure I want

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:36.783
an operating system for life.

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:40.500
Still, Eden Life represents the next stage

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.213
in Nigeria's tech evolution.

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:45.540
The country has a
seemingly infinite number

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:47.400
of mobile payment companies,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.563
and of course, crypto everything.

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:54.213
What it needs now are new
ideas and lots of them.

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:58.260
So like Andela, Flutterwave,

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:02.070
I mean, had huge international investors,

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:04.920
are those sort of one-offs
or now those investors

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.790
have seen the success here
and that's brought in more

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:09.600
of is it still really hard

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:12.690
to get international capital for startups?

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:15.330
It's definitely easier
than it was in 2014.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:18.030
Yeah, I'll say that by
orders of magnitude, right?

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:18.930
For multiple reasons.

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:22.560
One is the amount of talents that's here.

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:25.560
And so we're seeing a lot
more startups get funded.

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:27.723
The flywheel is definitely spinning.

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:34.890
The Andela mafia is a lot

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.393
like the PayPal mafia,

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:41.403
but with less ketamine and
fomenting of civil unrest.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:44.970
Nonetheless, it seemed
like fun to reminisce

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:47.280
with the Nigerian coding kingpins

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:50.610
over spicy goat pepper stew.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:53.370
The two close friends
met at school in Canada,

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:55.920
then came back to Lagos in 2013

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:58.170
to chase their startup dreams.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:18:59.550
So how old were you when you met then

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:00.820
like 16 or something?
16.

00:19:00.820 --> 00:19:02.340
Okay, yeah, so what'd you think?

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:07.290
Yeah, so my first impression of E was

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:09.190
why is this guy so grown up right now?

00:19:11.978 --> 00:19:14.640
He was always helping
people with their essays.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:16.173
Yeah, I really love essays.

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:21.393
To be fair, the Russian
kids were paying me, so.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:26.640
When you told your parents
you were all coming back

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.518
to do this thing after
everybody's gotten to Canada,

00:19:29.518 --> 00:19:33.120
everyone was okay or they
tried to talk you out of it?

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:35.727
Mine was an all-out war
'cause you know, keep in mind,

00:19:35.727 --> 00:19:37.890
I was already here, right?

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:40.110
They're just like, "You
should've tried harder

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:41.220
to find a job.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:43.140
What you mean you can't find a job?"

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:46.837
For me, it was just, I was ready.

00:19:46.837 --> 00:19:48.540
I was ready, I was like,
yeah, this is the adventure

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:49.800
of a lifetime.

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:50.970
Everybody had to sleep on the floor

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:53.760
'cause we couldn't afford a bed.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:55.170
So it was real hustle.

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:56.883
Proper startup life, man.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:58.673
It was really bad.

00:19:58.673 --> 00:20:00.250
Back then I used to drink a lot.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:04.080
We used to do something
called Breakfast of Champions,

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:07.920
a vodka shot and some Red Bull.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:09.547
I've had this breakfast
of champions a few times.

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:17.220
Was it scary or was it, like
you said it was just exciting

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:19.110
and you just had to see it through?

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:21.450
Well, to be fair,

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:24.918
this man right here is
a very sacrificial man

00:20:24.918 --> 00:20:29.918
and he put in a lot to
make the transition smooth.

00:20:30.115 --> 00:20:34.140
I try to keep all the
scary parts away from it.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:34.973
It's fun.

00:20:41.010 --> 00:20:42.270
The shorthand for what these men

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:44.940
are talking about is passion.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:47.070
Did you go to this facility?
Yeah.

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:49.650
It's what drove them to
build their companies

00:20:49.650 --> 00:20:51.510
and it's what's driving them now to try

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:53.913
and set Nigeria on a new course.

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:58.140
And the good news is that
if Nigeria has anything

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:00.723
in ample quantity, it's passion.

00:21:12.204 --> 00:21:13.980
Good evening, ladies and gentlemen.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.203
Welcome to Bogobiri open mic night.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:19.650
Any kind of talent, please let me know

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.203
and we will get your name on the list.

00:21:23.250 --> 00:21:25.080
It's Thursday night in Lagos.

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:27.270
I'm at the Bogobiri house. All week

00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:29.370
people have been telling me to come here,

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:32.430
see some spoken words, some
music, all kinds of stuff,

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:33.753
so I'm going to go in.

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:38.467
Please make some noise for
Senior Boy in the building.

00:21:56.003 --> 00:21:59.763
As with any open mic night,
there's a range of quality.

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:08.463
But the best acts are
face-meltingly great.

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:16.653
That greatness has made Nigerian
music a global phenomenon,

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:19.770
offering proof of the massive stage

00:22:19.770 --> 00:22:21.723
on which Nigeria can play.

00:22:30.678 --> 00:22:32.566
Thank you so much.

00:22:38.657 --> 00:22:40.920
But to really talk about Nigeria

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:42.660
as a cultural force,

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:46.503
we have to talk about
one extraordinary man.

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:53.820
The charismatic, controversial

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:57.180
and legendary musician Fela Kuti,

00:22:57.180 --> 00:23:00.573
who kicked off the Afrobeat
movement in the 1960s.

00:23:01.470 --> 00:23:06.300
Fela merged Nigerian sound
with jazz, soul, and the blues.

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:08.520
He sang about love and life

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:10.683
and also about the plight of his people.

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:13.350
Fela went right at the government

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:16.860
and what he saw as its
corruption and neglect.

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:20.973
He was an artist and a revolutionary
all rolled up into one.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:25.920
People still come to
pay tribute at this club

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:28.860
that acts as a kind of shrine to Fela.

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:31.890
Here you can bask in the
aura of the great man

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:34.143
and peruse various memorabilia.

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:38.973
I would comment on this exhibit,
but this is a family show.

00:23:45.296 --> 00:23:48.510
All right man, Clinton is my
name, I'm on the microphone.

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:51.780
Let him know we don't play
right there, let's go.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:54.390
What Kuti started with Afrobeat,

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.433
has since morphed into Afrobeats,

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:01.083
a glorious profusion of
West African pop styles.

00:24:02.490 --> 00:24:05.190
These are the sounds of the
country heard on every street

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:08.613
corner in every club and in every home.

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:12.183
And this music has washed
over the entire world,

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:17.337
turning Nigerian artists like Burna Boy

00:24:17.337 --> 00:24:21.197
and Wizkid into global icons.
Everybody sing.

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:28.050
One of the heavy hitters of the scene

00:24:28.050 --> 00:24:29.970
is Banky Wellington,

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:32.943
a singer and rapper turned
entertainment mogul.

00:24:34.136 --> 00:24:36.770
♪ Yeah, you are far from the regular ♪

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:38.700
So it started as me as an artist

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:42.090
to me starting a record label
and discovering other artists

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:45.660
and producers and DJs and
music business executives

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:47.910
all kind of got their start with us.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.940
And then over the years
we've morphed into movies.

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:51.780
I'm a filmmaker.

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:53.880
I've co-executive produced some films

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:55.403
that have done pretty well.
You have.

00:24:59.079 --> 00:25:02.490
You and your wife seem like
the celebrity couple of Nigeria

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:03.960
for what I could gather, right?

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:05.160
I'll let you see that.

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:10.230
Banky believes in Nigeria,

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:12.090
which is why he came back to live here

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:14.730
after going to university in New York.

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:16.890
He's even run for political office,

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:20.580
hoping to create more
opportunities for young people.

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:22.650
But not everyone who leaves Nigeria

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:25.170
is quite so eager to return.

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:29.580
I remember when I told my folks
that I wanted to move back

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.880
to Nigeria 'cause my parents
don't live here anymore.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:35.490
I mean, they haven't lived
here for a few decades.

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:37.357
I remember my folks said,

00:25:37.357 --> 00:25:39.300
"You want to sing, sing in New York City.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:41.640
There people are singing there
like what are you going back

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:42.857
to Nigeria to do?"

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:47.280
A lot of noise has been made about

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:50.220
the japa syndrome.
What is that?

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.680
Oh, japa is when people leave,

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:55.530
they go for other opportunities
outside of the country

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:59.673
and we've seen quite a few
of our bright minds do that.

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:07.713
Nigeria suffers from a deep brain drain,

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:10.620
holding back the country's tech scene

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:12.393
from reaching its full potential.

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:15.960
Increasingly, though, Nigerians

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:18.090
who have made it overseas are returning

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:21.000
with their skills and their money,

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:22.560
sometimes permanently,

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:24.963
sometimes in more of a hybrid fashion.

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:28.350
One of these people is Kunle,

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:30.963
a deal maker with
Capricorn Investment Group.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:34.050
Kunle grew up in Nigeria

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:37.740
and has made Africa his
focus as an investor.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:41.610
So did you guys grow up pretty
middle class, upper class?

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:43.740
You know, I think from the
perspective that I look at it,

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:45.374
I would say middle class,

00:26:45.374 --> 00:26:48.510
but I think my parents made
a lot of sacrifices for us

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:51.060
and I only know this looking back.

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:52.680
I studied chemical engineering

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:55.233
and had the opportunity to
go to the US to do that.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:26:59.250
Did you ever think you'd be coming back

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:02.310
to Africa to build startups?

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:06.180
No, but I have about 30+ plus companies

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:08.730
that I've invested in
now, not just in Nigeria,

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:10.203
but across the continent.

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:19.920
Kunle brought me three
hours north of Lagos

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:23.370
to Ibadan, the capital of Oye State.

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:24.240
This is cool, man.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:26.760
It's kind of so different than Lagos.

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:29.490
Seems like one notch down frenetic.

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:31.803
Yeah, definitely one notch down.

00:27:34.020 --> 00:27:35.790
We had two missions,

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:39.300
to meet one of Kunle's
friends, a local politician,

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:41.283
and to visit Kunle grandmother.

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:44.493
First though, we'd have to get gas.

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:50.493
That's not a scene we'd
usually put in the show.

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:56.333
Then again,

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:00.903
it usually doesn't look like this.

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:07.740
To understand what's going on here,

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:09.213
takes a bit of explaining.

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:13.770
Shortly after I arrived in Nigeria,

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:17.103
a new president, Bola
Ahmed Tinubu, took office.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:21.180
His first major act was to end

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:23.253
the government subsidies for gasoline.

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:28.473
This triggered an immediate
panic throughout the country.

00:28:29.700 --> 00:28:33.093
People rushed to gas stations
to try and beat price hikes.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:36.180
The gas stations were then barricaded

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:38.230
in a bid to control the situation

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:42.333
and simply filling up
your car became an ordeal.

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:47.580
The underlying problem here is
that Nigeria has lots of oil

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:49.503
but no refineries to process it.

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:54.300
Oil is exported, refined,
and then brought back,

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:57.513
with Nigeria, usually getting
the dregs of the production.

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:00.450
Africa's richest person.

00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:04.260
Aliko Dangote just paid
close to $20 billion

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:07.323
to build the first new modern
refinery in the country.

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:09.173
You're building

00:29:09.173 --> 00:29:10.710
one of the largest
refineries in the world?

00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:15.510
Yes, it is 650,000 barrel a day.

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:16.343
So there's hope

00:29:16.343 --> 00:29:19.083
that with the right
investments, things can improve.

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:23.883
But such examples are few and far between.

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:27.570
These economic woes make it even harder

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:29.043
for tech to flourish here.

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:32.100
And almost no one seems to believe

00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:34.250
that the government
can make things better,

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:38.927
except apparently, for
this man, Adebayo Akande.

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:41.700
I don't know if you're aware of this,

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:44.160
but some people are cynical
about the government

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:47.850
here, you know, and how much progress?

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:49.590
What do you mean we're
not aware about that?

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:50.654
Like what are you talking about?

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:55.470
Actually I was one of
those people four years ago

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:58.860
and I think we can be on the outside

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:01.430
and complain forever

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:04.623
or we can actually try to
come in and make a change.

00:30:07.890 --> 00:30:09.600
Bayo is a former entrepreneur

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:11.733
who decided to go into politics.

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:15.060
His mission has been to
modernize the government

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:18.450
of Oyo State and lay the
groundwork for tech companies

00:30:18.450 --> 00:30:20.460
to set up shop here.

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:24.090
We're very interested
in internet penetration.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:27.300
So we've laid fiber cables, fiber docks,

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:29.010
almost 1,000 over the past few years.

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:30.660
We'll probably do a couple thousand more.

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:33.330
Yeah, they always say the
second high speed internet

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:35.820
lights up in a country,
it's education improves,

00:30:35.820 --> 00:30:37.923
job prospects improve, all of that.

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:47.940
Bayo is also putting technology
to work fighting graft,

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:50.520
which many Nigerians say runs rampant

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:52.200
throughout the government here.

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:55.800
We were talking too about
the, if $12 billion goes

00:30:55.800 --> 00:30:58.200
to the construction of a new road,

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:01.140
only two billion actually
make it to the road

00:31:01.140 --> 00:31:03.870
after sort of money's changed hands.

00:31:03.870 --> 00:31:05.449
So what we've done here, for example,

00:31:05.449 --> 00:31:08.580
one of the first things
we did was every single

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:11.880
contract procurement should be put online.

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:13.770
The whole process is transparent

00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:16.530
and the monies to be
dispersed also online.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:21.513
So full information, transparency
across the entire process.

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:26.400
All this, Bayo hopes, can
make life a little better

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:29.280
and easier for everyday Nigerians

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:31.740
and help convince young tech entrepreneurs

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:34.143
that they should build their futures here.

00:31:37.800 --> 00:31:40.680
If you can just make life
a little better for them,

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:42.540
nobody wants to leave home.

00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:45.450
If they can, they would
stay here and thrive here,

00:31:45.450 --> 00:31:48.210
maybe earn from there, but live here,

00:31:48.210 --> 00:31:49.890
so it's policies, man.

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:52.650
I mean, I trust Bayo,

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:55.113
he seems nice and well-intentioned.

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:04.233
But for the real talk on the real Nigeria,

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:09.330
I knew where we had to go next.

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:11.700
Welcome to Ibadan, this
is my grandma's home.

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:13.050
Thank you.

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:14.490
Lots of fun memories here,

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:17.013
so let's go say hi to Grandma.

00:32:17.970 --> 00:32:18.903
Hello, Grandma.

00:32:20.204 --> 00:32:22.233
Aw, good to see you, Grandma.

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:25.680
This is Ashlee.
Hello.

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:27.450
Ashlee.
Welcome.

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:28.283
Thank you.

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:33.510
Mrs. Grace Omotayo Apampa
was born in Nigeria,

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:37.410
went to college in England,
and came back in 1959,

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:39.870
just a year before Independence.

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:40.703
Is that you?

00:32:42.149 --> 00:32:43.590
No, that's not.
No.

00:32:43.590 --> 00:32:45.750
She worked at the University of Nigeria's

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:49.560
Teaching Hospital while
raising several kids,

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:51.663
and she remains a mom at heart.

00:32:53.250 --> 00:32:55.140
The gentleman, let them eat it now.

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:57.180
You only give them one, one.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:59.100
Grandma, they're busy, that's why.

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:00.570
They're busy with the cameras.

00:33:00.570 --> 00:33:02.370
Tell them to help themselves then.

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:05.013
Yes, please help yourselves.
We will.

00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:10.290
My grandchildren are all over,

00:33:10.290 --> 00:33:11.520
they're scattered all over.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:12.510
Are all over the world?

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:14.847
I'm happy that they're all over the world,

00:33:14.847 --> 00:33:16.503
but I miss them.
Yeah.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:24.660
Madam Grace has witnessed generations

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:27.750
of change here, but
still keeps a sharp eye

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:29.520
on current events.

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:33.240
I just learned about the
refinery on this visit.

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:37.140
I mean, the country should
be refining its own oil.

00:33:37.140 --> 00:33:38.130
Yes.

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:42.090
We send the crude oil,
we sell it to foreigners.

00:33:42.090 --> 00:33:44.550
They return it to us at a exorbitant rate.

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:46.050
Yeah, it's crazy.
It's crazy.

00:33:46.050 --> 00:33:50.520
Yeah, how do you feel
about the young, the youth,

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:54.030
the youngsters and do they give you hope

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:56.820
for where the country is heading?

00:33:56.820 --> 00:34:00.000
I think the future is bright for them,

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:02.070
but we have to come together.

00:34:02.070 --> 00:34:04.230
We have to come home,
do this thing together.

00:34:04.230 --> 00:34:05.580
Together, yeah.

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:06.663
Not running away.

00:34:06.663 --> 00:34:09.810
We have to be together, stick and do it.

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:10.800
She's talking to you.

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:12.250
Yeah, she is talking to me.

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:15.289
I accept the challenge.

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:24.030
Back in Lagos,

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:27.150
you can find a number of
startups that Kunle has backed

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:29.490
with an eye toward making real change

00:34:29.490 --> 00:34:31.203
for everyday Nigerians.

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:37.320
Tech investors have claimed
such noble intentions

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:39.390
since at least the stone age.

00:34:39.390 --> 00:34:44.390
Hello, my friends,
welcome to Ibibio Class.

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:46.893
But here in Nigeria,
there's a case to be made

00:34:46.893 --> 00:34:50.040
that tech might actually
make things better.

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:53.707
V, W, X, Y and zet.

00:34:54.723 --> 00:34:56.550
Take Teesas.

00:34:56.550 --> 00:35:00.690
They make an education app
and low-cost tablet for kids,

00:35:00.690 --> 00:35:03.670
and also run in-person
learning centers like this one

00:35:04.830 --> 00:35:07.740
with the goal of making
quality education available

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:09.783
for even the poorest households.

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:15.330
It was started by Osayi,

00:35:15.330 --> 00:35:18.480
a Nigerian and former Exxon executive.

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:23.310
Today's May 27th and at
apparently is Children's Day

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:25.710
and is this like a Africa-wide holiday

00:35:25.710 --> 00:35:27.630
or this is a Nigerian?

00:35:27.630 --> 00:35:30.191
So it's mainly Nigerian.

00:35:30.191 --> 00:35:33.180
We create a special day for the children

00:35:33.180 --> 00:35:35.780
where we just celebrate
them, give them a good time.

00:35:37.100 --> 00:35:39.050
Now we're going to stretch ourselves.

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:45.630
The way Osayi sees it,

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:49.830
the education in Nigerian public
schools is barely adequate.

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:51.660
Teesas aims to fill in the gaps

00:35:51.660 --> 00:35:53.430
and get kids ready for college

00:35:53.430 --> 00:35:56.940
and life all for just
a few dollars a month.

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:58.770
We're focused on the whole child,

00:35:58.770 --> 00:35:59.603
not just academics.

00:35:59.603 --> 00:36:01.200
Yes, we prepare them for exams,

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:04.350
but we also want 'em to
start developing core values

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:06.870
that would help them
throughout their entire lives,

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:08.610
like being proactive, right?

00:36:08.610 --> 00:36:11.653
Social emotional learning,
can you say that?

00:36:11.653 --> 00:36:13.960
Social emotional learning.

00:36:13.960 --> 00:36:15.717
Being here they have access to the best

00:36:15.717 --> 00:36:17.670
and the brightest tutors

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:20.164
and learning coaches across the country.

00:36:20.164 --> 00:36:21.717
Happy Children's Day!

00:36:21.717 --> 00:36:22.550
Happy Children's Day!

00:36:33.930 --> 00:36:35.340
Another Kunle-backed company

00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:38.553
trying to make things better
in Nigeria is Farmz2U.

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:44.580
It's run by Aisha Raheem,

00:36:44.580 --> 00:36:47.400
who spent a few years
working in finance in London

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:50.055
before returning to her homeland.

00:36:50.055 --> 00:36:51.173
I do go back and forth

00:36:51.173 --> 00:36:53.430
because I also naturalized in England

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:55.065
so they're both my homes

00:36:55.065 --> 00:37:00.000
and the two cities I've lived
long-term, Lagos, London.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:01.800
Yeah.
Two mad houses,

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:06.543
but lots of entrepreneurial
spirit, so I love those cities.

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:08.413
Like Teesas,

00:37:08.413 --> 00:37:13.413
Farmz2U is not super flashy,
but it is incredibly useful.

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:16.140
The company makes software for farmers,

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:19.560
helping them figure out what
types of crops buyers want,

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:22.380
when the best time is to sell a harvest,

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:24.420
and even how to get loans.

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:26.460
You've probably got people who have,

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:29.340
they're family's been farming
for many, many, many years

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:32.730
and then you're kind of
coming in with some new ideas

00:37:32.730 --> 00:37:35.910
and then also you have to get
the technology in their hands.

00:37:35.910 --> 00:37:37.560
In terms of even convincing them

00:37:37.560 --> 00:37:39.630
and getting them to think about things

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:42.360
in a more technology-oriented way,

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:43.950
it's actually something
they're more open to

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:46.143
because of the impact of climate change.

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:52.320
The rains are coming later,
they're coming heavier.

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:55.740
So for instance, last year
in Nigeria we experienced

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:57.060
one of the heaviest floods

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:58.800
that we've seen in the last decade

00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:00.750
and this is why they're more receptive

00:38:00.750 --> 00:38:03.210
and really trying to understand
how can I do things better

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:05.373
because they're playing very thin origins.

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:09.030
Needless to say, Nigerian
farming logistics

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:11.133
is not the easiest business to be in.

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:16.413
But for Aisha, the challenges
are kind of the point.

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:22.018
You see a pothole on the
road, that's an opportunity.

00:38:22.018 --> 00:38:26.190
There isn't constant light,
that is an opportunity.

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:29.640
There is corruption as the, you know,

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:32.670
traditional sense, that's opportunity.

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:33.840
What is actually corruption

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:35.580
is just that systems are not in place

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:37.350
to capture certain activities

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:39.240
because what might be
called corruption in Nigeria

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:41.190
might be called lobbying in the US.

00:38:41.190 --> 00:38:43.440
What might be called a bribe in Nigeria

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:46.050
might be called a service fee in the UK.

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:48.090
What is in Nigeria is a systems problem.

00:38:48.090 --> 00:38:49.680
It's not a people problem,

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:52.330
it's systems and processes
that needs to be in place.

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:01.140
By now I was feeling so inspired

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:03.840
by the possibilities of Nigerian tech,

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:06.753
I felt like I wanted to
get involved personally.

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:13.440
So I got myself an
entry-level job at a startup

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:16.323
delivering food to
customers all over Lagos.

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:20.913
Little did I know how intense
this job would get later on.

00:39:21.912 --> 00:39:23.978
Over here we have the spaghetti.

00:39:23.978 --> 00:39:26.313
We have spaghetti here.
And oil.

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:30.660
My new employer is a
company called Grocedy,

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:34.563
which aims to sell staple foods
at the lowest possible cost.

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:38.250
They work directly with
suppliers like Aisha's company

00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:40.800
Farmz2U to buy food in bulk

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:43.893
and pass on the savings
to low-income Lagosians.

00:39:44.872 --> 00:39:46.470
I give the order.

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:48.500
So this is the app.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:52.230
My tenure at Grocedy
started out well enough.

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:54.199
We're doing the gold order.
It's a good basket.

00:39:54.199 --> 00:39:55.176
All right.

00:39:57.090 --> 00:39:59.250
You pack the food at a warehouse.

00:39:59.250 --> 00:40:00.960
All right, I'll follow you.

00:40:00.960 --> 00:40:02.703
You pop it in a delivery vehicle.

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:08.463
Teamwork makes the dream work.
Yeah.

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:12.360
Then you drive through the crazy streets

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:15.120
of Lagos to reach your hungry customer.

00:40:15.120 --> 00:40:18.357
Lagos is a kind of a very busy place.

00:40:18.357 --> 00:40:20.463
Sometimes it is stressful,

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:24.063
traffic, so many things.

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:27.603
You listen to podcasts, what do you?

00:40:31.590 --> 00:40:33.610
Finally you arrive at the customer

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:37.320
where, because you have
a film crew with you,

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:38.823
all hell breaks loose.

00:40:43.350 --> 00:40:45.000
We'd already had a few encounters

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:46.653
with the so-called Area Boys,

00:40:48.540 --> 00:40:50.823
Lagos' neighborhood enforcers.

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:57.150
But these guys were
particularly aggressive

00:40:57.150 --> 00:40:58.773
with their threats of violence.

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:06.150
All right, our arrival
wasn't that appreciated.

00:41:06.150 --> 00:41:07.620
Little money was exchanged I think

00:41:07.620 --> 00:41:10.743
and now we're going to
go deliver the groceries.

00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:14.210
Well, I'm sorry we made your day-

00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:17.340
No problem.
More hectic.

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:19.530
All right, man, it was nice meeting you.

00:41:19.530 --> 00:41:21.270
Enjoy the groceries.

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:22.103
Cheers.

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:25.380
Sadly, that was the end of my career

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:27.003
as a Nigerian delivery man.

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:36.930
To ease the pain of my failure,

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:39.090
Kunle and his friend Michael took me

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:40.743
for a relaxing boat ride.

00:41:41.820 --> 00:41:44.430
Destination, Ilashe Beach,

00:41:44.430 --> 00:41:46.540
a resort area just outside the city

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:50.523
where chill vibes can be found at last.

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:55.590
People from Lagos come
out here just to relax?

00:41:55.590 --> 00:41:56.940
Yeah, to relax.

00:41:56.940 --> 00:42:00.483
To escape the chaos of the actual city.

00:42:01.500 --> 00:42:03.360
Tell me how you guys met.

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:07.980
Goes as far back as the
primary school days, I reckon,

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:10.050
before he moved to the States

00:42:10.050 --> 00:42:13.260
and I've been here just
keeping things going, I guess.

00:42:13.260 --> 00:42:15.300
But you know, everybody, he
is got all the connections

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:16.133
'cause he's stayed.

00:42:16.133 --> 00:42:17.860
If there's anyone that's plugged in.

00:42:22.350 --> 00:42:23.550
Michael has made the most

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:25.083
of his decision to stay.

00:42:26.550 --> 00:42:29.730
He now runs a business as
a kind of high-end fixer

00:42:29.730 --> 00:42:32.103
and guide to all things Lagos.

00:42:33.180 --> 00:42:34.620
Shout outs to the Afrobeats artists.

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:38.220
They're making Africa,
Nigeria, Lagos so popular.

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:40.350
Everyone is trying to come back here.

00:42:40.350 --> 00:42:42.090
How much do you get when you come back?

00:42:42.090 --> 00:42:44.553
People try to recruit you to come home?

00:42:45.960 --> 00:42:49.440
I think it's a mutual
understanding that, you know,

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:53.910
maybe we playfully use it,
but people understand the whys

00:42:53.910 --> 00:42:57.030
I think whether you stay
here or you live abroad,

00:42:57.030 --> 00:43:00.180
our goal is just to make Lagos, Nigeria,

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:02.550
a better home for all of us.

00:43:02.550 --> 00:43:06.570
Community, food, health, education.

00:43:06.570 --> 00:43:09.540
I think if those basic
infrastructures existed,

00:43:09.540 --> 00:43:11.250
you see less people wanting to move.

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:12.510
They'll still struggle,

00:43:12.510 --> 00:43:15.540
but they'll know that like
the basic needs are met

00:43:15.540 --> 00:43:17.490
and they would much
rather want to stay here

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:18.811
than go and live somewhere else.

00:43:18.811 --> 00:43:19.981
Because it's home at the end of the day.

00:43:19.981 --> 00:43:20.981
Yeah, it is.

00:43:25.927 --> 00:43:28.950
Kunle and Michael continued
telling me about their many

00:43:28.950 --> 00:43:30.670
prospective business ventures

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:36.060
and their hopes and
dreams for their country.

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:37.947
Good to see you, man, thank you.

00:43:37.947 --> 00:43:39.720
Yeah, all right, yeah,

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:41.880
Seeing Nigeria through their eyes,

00:43:41.880 --> 00:43:44.403
it's easy to imagine a bright future.

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:48.150
The truth, at least for me,

00:43:48.150 --> 00:43:51.050
is that there's something
intoxicating about this country.

00:43:52.230 --> 00:43:54.690
Its problems are so obvious.

00:43:54.690 --> 00:43:56.890
Most of the country's
people are struggling.

00:43:58.290 --> 00:44:00.333
There's dysfunction everywhere.

00:44:01.470 --> 00:44:04.020
So many of Nigeria's
best and brightest people

00:44:04.020 --> 00:44:06.393
have fled for brighter
prospects elsewhere.

00:44:08.100 --> 00:44:10.683
But in the end, there's that energy.

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:15.153
The people who've stayed
feel it and thrive on it.

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:18.153
Anyone visiting will feel it too.

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:21.300
It's a kind of vibrating hum

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:25.833
that should be and could be
the backdrop for great things.

